<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="FCO5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Foundational Concepts and Objectives</title>
  
<subsection xml:id="subsec-p5">
  <title>Purpose</title>
  <p> The purpose of this lab is to explore how quantitative methods can be used to describe variation within a biological population. 
    By measuring seed weights from a sample of bean seeds, students will learn how to collect representative data, construct histograms, and calculate key descriptive statistics — including mean, variance, standard deviation, and confidence intervals. 
    These tools will help students interpret patterns of variation and understand how traits like seed weight relate to ecological and evolutionary processes such as resource allocation and reproductive strategy.
  </p>
</subsection>    

<subsection xml:id="subsec-obj5">
  <title>Learning Objectives</title>
  
  <p> You will: </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 5.1:</term> Identify potential sources of bias when collecting a sample from a biological population and apply strategies to ensure random and representative sampling 
                (e.g., bean seed mixing and selection).
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 5.2:</term> Describe a statistical population using calculated measures of central tendency <term>(mean, median, mode)</term> and variation <term>(range, variance, standard deviation, standard error, confidence interval)</term>. 
                Students should be able to compute these metrics by hand using sample data.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 5.3:</term> Explain the role of descriptive statistics in ecological research, particularly in understanding variation, estimating population parameters, and evaluating the reliability of sample-based conclusions.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 5.4:</term> Construct a histogram by hand to visualize the distribution of a biological trait (e.g., seed weight) and interpret its shape in the context of ecological variation and reproductive strategy.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</subsection>

